/**
 * @mainpage ManuMesh Developer Reference
 *
 * ManuMesh is a C++17 kernel for triangle-surface meshes. Its current
 * production pipeline covers mesh validation and topology queries, geometric
 * analysis, deterministic feature-curve extraction, feature-aware quadric
 * error simplification, and STL/OBJ exchange. Point-cloud reconstruction,
 * B-Rep feature trees, solid modeling, and volumetric meshing are outside the
 * scope of this codebase.
 *
 * @section architecture Architecture
 *
 * The public SDK in `include/` owns stable data types and entry points. The
 * implementation in `src/` is split into reusable geometry foundations and
 * two algorithm pipelines:
 *
 * - @ref manumesh_core stores triangle meshes, validates input, and builds
 *   immutable topology.
 * - @ref manumesh_common provides predicates, neighborhood queries, distance
 *   indices, and broad-phase spatial acceleration.
 * - @ref manumesh_feature_detection converts local hard and weak evidence into
 *   a coherent feature graph, loops, components, and surface patches.
 * - @ref manumesh_simplification ranks edge collapses with QEM/line quadrics,
 *   then applies topology, geometry, feature, boundary, texture, and error
 *   guards before modifying the mesh.
 * - @ref manumesh_analysis, @ref manumesh_mesh_edit, and @ref manumesh_io
 *   provide cross-pipeline analysis, edit-time topology, and file exchange.
 * - @ref manumesh_c_api exposes a size-aware ABI that never lets C++
 *   exceptions cross the language boundary.
 *
 * @section feature_pipeline Feature-detection pipeline
 *
 * The detector operates on triangle or polygon-derived triangle surface
 * meshes. It first records boundary, non-manifold, oriented-dihedral,
 * normal-tensor, and optional smooth-curvature evidence. The weak channels are
 * sampling-scale aware and require directional or multiscale persistence.
 * Evidence is materialized as a graph; cleanup and consolidation remove weak
 * spurs, bridge compatible gaps, and organize junction branches. Tracing,
 * cycle recovery, primitive fitting, component confidence, and patch
 * segmentation consume that graph rather than recomputing local evidence.
 *
 * @failuremodes Irregular neighborhoods can make curvature fits rank
 * deficient; inconsistent winding can make the signed dihedral unknown; weak
 * thresholds can create a dense fragmented graph; and a single local tangent
 * cannot represent every tight multi-branch junction. Diagnostics in
 * `FeatureAnalysis` make these cases observable.
 *
 * @section simplify_pipeline Simplification pipeline
 *
 * The simplifier accumulates face-plane quadrics and optional boundary,
 * feature-curve, and line regularizers. Candidate placement follows the
 * Garland-Heckbert solve with rank-aware edge/end-point fallbacks. QEM is only
 * the ranking objective: an accepted collapse must also satisfy the extended
 * link condition, boundary policy, normal and triangle-quality bounds, local
 * self-intersection and distance budgets, feature-curve budgets, and optional
 * UV chart checks. Queue versions invalidate stale candidates after each
 * local topology edit. A fixed-topology refinement pass may improve triangle
 * quality after the target face budget is reached.
 *
 * @invariants Active faces always reference active vertices; dynamic incidence
 * caches are updated atomically with face rewrites; protected feature and
 * boundary constraints are checked before mutation; and every accepted
 * placement has a precomputed legality and texture plan.
 *
 * @section docs Documentation layout
 *
 * `docs/doxygen/html/index.html` is generated by the `docs-api` CMake target
 * and is intentionally not versioned. Design records, guides, paper PDFs, and
 * historical generated notes live under `documentation/`; they are supporting
 * engineering material rather than Doxygen input.
 */
